// THE CLOUD LAYER'S SHADOW AS A RECEIVER SEES IT — one text, compiled by every shader that shades a
// surface with the sun.
//
// Why it is a header and not five copies. The engine shades the same materials in the same scene through
// two paths a scene picks with a single enum field, and the deferred path additionally draws several
// classes of geometry FORWARD over its own composite (skinned meshes, glass, custom-shader meshes) plus
// a terrain that has never gone through either mesh shader. While this wrapper lived inside
// Programs/Deferred/DeferredLighting.shader it was the ONLY place in the tree that read the map: every
// forward-shaded scene, every skinned mesh, every piece of glass and every terrain stood in full sun
// under a cloud deck that was shading the ground beside it. Measured on the ground of Clouds_Showcase,
// clouds on: 64.93/255 through the deferred path against 109.11/255 through the forward one — a factor
// of 1.68, and the whole of what remained between the two paths after Р16 and Р20 shared the ambient and
// the direct-light BRDF. The cure is the one those tasks used: not a test that the copies still agree,
// but a single copy that cannot disagree.
//
// It is split from Common/CloudShadowMap.glslh rather than living inside it, and the split is forced
// rather than stylistic: that file is included by the PRODUCER too, and the producer declares
// `u_CloudShadowMap` as a writeonly image2D it stores into. A receiver function naming the same symbol
// as a sampler2D cannot be in the file the producer compiles.
//
// WHAT THE INCLUDER OWES IT — the same contract Mesh/AmbientIBL.glslh has with its three environment
// bindings. This file declares no binding of its own, because the slot numbers differ between the
// consumers (the deferred composite is a fullscreen material with its own layout; the mesh shaders share
// one with the G-buffer pass; the terrain has a small layout of its own). The includer must declare, at
// whatever slots are free in ITS layout:
//
//     Uniform(a) sampler2D u_CloudShadowMap;
//     Uniform(b) CloudShadowUB {
//         mat4 u_CloudShadowWorldToMap;
//         vec4 u_CloudShadowParams;
//     };
//
// The NAMES are what has to match, and they do because every material binds by name — see
// Graphic::CloudShadowBind, which is the single C++ writer of both.

#ifndef CLOUD_SHADOW_RECEIVER_GLSLH
#define CLOUD_SHADOW_RECEIVER_GLSLH

// The map's RECONSTRUCTION half — the same text its producer compiles, so the encode and the decode
// cannot drift apart. Its march stays inert here because a receiver defines no
// CLOUD_SHADOW_SAMPLE_EXTINCTION: there is no cloud field, no noise volume and no profile table anywhere
// near a lighting pass.
#include <Common/CloudShadowMap.glslh>

// 1 = full sun, 0 = fully shaded by cloud. A MULTIPLIER on the direct sun term, never a replacement for
// the cascades: they occlude the sun with opaque geometry, this occludes it with a volume, and a surface
// can be in either, both or neither.
//
// ONE FETCH, NO CASCADE, NO DEPTH COMPARE. The map's texel is not an occluder distance — it is
// (frontDepthKm, meanExtinctionPerKm, maxOpticalDepth), and Common/CloudShadowMap.glslh turns that triple
// into a transmittance at whatever depth this receiver sits at. That is what makes it correct for a
// mountain top INSIDE the layer as well as for the ground under it.
//
// ONLY THE DIRECTIONAL TERM. The ambient is the whole sky dome, which a cloud deck occludes with a
// different geometry (a hemisphere, not a direction) and which UE answers with a separate Sky AO volume.
// Folding a directional occlusion into an omnidirectional term would darken the shaded side of every
// object under a cloud by an amount nothing in the world justifies.
//
// Costs one uniform compare in every scene that has no clouds — the sampler is then on its dummy image
// and is never touched.
float CloudShadowFactor(vec3 worldPos)
{
	if (u_CloudShadowParams.y < 0.5)
		return 1.0;

	// The perspective divide is written out even though the map's projection is ORTHOGRAPHIC and its w is
	// identically 1: the day a consumer is handed a different projection this line is already correct,
	// and the divide costs nothing against the texture fetch below it.
	vec4 mapClip = u_CloudShadowWorldToMap * vec4(worldPos, 1.0);
	mapClip     /= max(mapClip.w, 1e-6);
	vec2 uv      = CloudShadowClipToUv(mapClip.xy);

	// Outside the covered square there is no answer, and inventing one is worse than saying so: the
	// shadow is faded out over the last few texels so the map's boundary is a gradient rather than a
	// straight line of light across the terrain. See CLOUD_SHADOWMAP_BORDER_FADE_KM.
	float border = CloudShadowBorderFade(uv, u_CloudShadowParams.z);
	if (border <= 0.0)
		return 1.0;

	float depthKm       = CloudShadowDepthKm(mapClip.z, u_CloudShadowParams.x);
	float transmittance = CloudShadowTransmittance(texture(u_CloudShadowMap, uv).rgb, depthKm,
	                                               u_CloudShadowParams.w);
	return mix(1.0, transmittance, border);
}

#endif // CLOUD_SHADOW_RECEIVER_GLSLH
